q2.content:
### Three requirements must hold at the same time

First check shape **(2,2)**, then $R^TR\approx I$ and $\det R\approx1$. Orthogonality means perpendicular unit axes and preservation of length. The determinant requirement excludes reflections.

~~~python
def valid_so2(candidate):
    if not isinstance(candidate, np.ndarray) or candidate.shape != (2, 2):
        return False
    if not np.issubdtype(candidate.dtype, np.number) or np.iscomplexobj(candidate):
        return False
    if not np.isfinite(candidate).all():
        return False
    return bool(
        np.allclose(candidate.T @ candidate, np.eye(2), atol=1e-8, rtol=1e-7)
        and np.isclose(np.linalg.det(candidate), 1.0, atol=1e-8, rtol=1e-7)
    )
~~~

The tolerances are own numeric choices, not documented limits for private tests. Exact equality is unsuitable for trigonometric floating point. The matrix $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$ gives $R^TR=I$, but $\det R=-1$ and should be rejected. The matrix $\operatorname{diag}(2,0.5)$ has determinant 1, but $R^TR=\operatorname{diag}(4,0.25)\ne I$ and should also be rejected. Test both; one of the requirements alone is insufficient.

q1.content:
### Keep the axis that is rotated about

From [week 3](/egb339/uker/uke-3) and [SO(3)](/egb339/temaer/so-3-rotation-matrices), with $c=\cos\theta$, $s=\sin\theta$:

$$
R_x=\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\0&c&-s\\0&s&c\end{bmatrix},\quad
R_y=\begin{bmatrix}c&0&s\\0&1&0\\-s&0&c\end{bmatrix},\quad
R_z=\begin{bmatrix}c&-s&0\\s&c&0\\0&0&1\end{bmatrix}.
$$

~~~python
import numpy as np

def rotation_matrix(axis, angle):
    c, s = np.cos(angle), np.sin(angle)
    if axis == "x":
        return np.array([[1, 0, 0], [0, c, -s], [0, s, c]], dtype=float)
    if axis == "y":
        return np.array([[c, 0, s], [0, 1, 0], [-s, 0, c]], dtype=float)
    if axis == "z":
        return np.array([[c, -s, 0], [s, c, 0], [0, 0, 1]], dtype=float)
    raise ValueError("Aksen må være x, y eller z")
~~~

The starter file requires **rotation_matrix(axis, angle)** and an SO(3) matrix. For $R_y(\pi/2)$, $(1,0,0)$ becomes $(0,0,-1)$; this checks the sign according to the right-hand rule. Test all axes at 0, $\pi/2$ and a negative angle, as well as $R^TR\approx I$ and determinant 1.
